Secure Code Generation
Research (7/24)
This paper (arXiv:2506.23034) is a comprehensive empirical study of how often LLMs write insecure code, whether self-generated vulnerability hints can prevent it, and whether LLMs can repair vulnerabilities after the fact when given feedback. In short: LLMs are consistently insecure by default, hints/feedback help but only when they're specific and well-targeted, and the model's instruction-following ability matters more than its size.

Overview
Motivation / gap the paper identifies (Section 1-2)
Prior work on LLM code security mostly tested a narrow set of big proprietary models (GPT-3.5/4, Copilot); open-weight and smaller-scale models were understudied despite growing real-world use.
Two established comparisons: Pearce et al. found ~40% of Copilot's code across 18 CWE types was vulnerable; Siddiq & Santos (creators of the SecurityEval benchmark) found 68-74% vulnerable code from InCoder/Copilot. This paper positions itself as filling the "broader range of models" gap those studies left open.
Study design, 3 dimensions (Section 3, Figure 1)
Do LLMs generate vulnerable code with plain prompts? (baseline)
Can self-generated vulnerability hints prevent it proactively?
Can LLMs repair vulnerable code post-hoc when given feedback?
Datasets (Table 1, Section 4)
SecCodePLT: 1,071 questions (after excluding 274 unsupported by CodeQL), covering 21 CWE types, synthesized via mutation from expert-crafted seeds.
SecurityEval: 121 questions, covering a much broader 69 CWE types, drawn from real-world code + expert-authored scenarios.
16 CWE types overlap between the two, but SecurityEval covers 53 additional types SecCodePLT doesn't, which is why models tend to look worse on SecurityEval (more diverse, unfamiliar vulnerability types).
Models tested (Table 2)
CodeLlama-Instruct (7B, 34B), Llama3.1-Instruct (8B), Llama3.2-Instruct (3B), StarCoder2-Instruct (15B), DeepSeek-Coder-V2-Lite-Instruct (16B), GPT-3.5-turbo, GPT-4o. Selected for diversity across architecture family, parameter scale (3B–34B), and open vs. closed weights.
Metrics (Section 4)
TarV-R (Target Vulnerability Rate): % of generated code containing the specific vulnerability the question was designed to test for.
AllV-R (All Vulnerability Rate): % of generated code containing any vulnerability, targeted or not. Lower = better for both.
Less summarized repeat ↓
RQ1. Do LLMs generate vulnerable code? (Section 5.1, Table 3)
Overall vulnerability rates ranged ~4%-27% depending on model/dataset. Average across models: SecCodePLT TarV-R 8.5%/AllV-R 13.2%; SecurityEval TarV-R 20.7%/AllV-R 33.4%, confirming the harder, more diverse benchmark produces worse results across the board.
GPT-4o had the highest AllV-R on SecCodePLT (18.0%) among all models tested, despite being the most capable model overall.
DeepSeekV2-16B swung dramatically between datasets: 4.2% TarV-R on SecCodePLT vs. 27.3% on SecurityEval, showing vulnerability performance is highly dataset/CWE-type dependent, not a fixed model trait.
Within-family scaling isn't a guarantee: CodeLlama-34B beat CodeLlama-7B (larger = safer, as expected), but GPT-4o was more vulnerable than GPT-3.5-turbo, and Llama3 models were more vulnerable than the older CodeLlama line. The authors flag this as an open question, unclear whether it's architecture, training data, or fine-tuning strategy driving it.
Code-optimized models (CodeLlama, StarCoder2) generally beat general-purpose models on SecCodePLT but lost that edge on SecurityEval's broader vulnerability set.
Vulnerability types were highly consistent across models, top-10 vulnerable CWE types overlapped heavily across all 8 models (only 11 unique types total), suggesting shared blind spots baked into training data/architecture rather than model-specific quirks (Figure 6).
RQ2. Do self-generated hints help? (Section 5.2, Tables 4-7)
Method: model lists its own top-5 predicted vulnerabilities before writing code, then that list is fed back in as guidance.
Results were mixed and model-dependent: GPT-4o dropped TarV-R by 12.4 points on both datasets; Llama3.1-8B dropped ~4-8 points; several other models barely moved or got slightly worse.
The paper isolates why: hints only helped when they (a) actually named the real target vulnerability ("Hints w. TarV" vs. "Hints w/o TarV" in Table 5) and (b) were precise/correct. When hints missed the target vulnerability, TarV-R increased for almost every model except Llama3.1-8B and GPT-4o, irrelevant hints actively mislead the model.
Hint precision varied hugely by model: GPT-4o's hints were 98.6% precise (verified via GPT-4o-as-judge, cross-checked against human raters at 95.65% precision/91.67% recall agreement); CodeLlama-7B's were only 46.5% precise.
A further experiment isolated "contextualized" hints (grounded in the specific coding task) vs. bare CWE definitions (Table 6, Table 7). Contextualized hints consistently outperformed plain definitions, especially on SecurityEval, e.g., GPT-4o's TarV-R dropped 17.3 points with contextualized hints vs. 15.7 with definitions alone.
Weaker-instruction-following models (CodeLlama-7B/34B) showed minimal improvement even with high-quality contextualized hints, suggesting a ceiling driven by the model's ability to act on information, not just receive it.
RQ3. Can LLMs repair their own vulnerable code? (Section 5.3, Table 8)
Two feedback types compared, both sourced from CodeQL: "direct feedback" (raw tool output: CWE ID + line number) vs. "explained feedback" (GPT-4o expands that into a full explanation + fix suggestions).
Explained feedback consistently beat direct feedback for models with strong instruction-following: GPT-4o's AllV-R dropped 28.1 points on SecurityEval under explained feedback (vs. also 28.1 under direct, a case where it maxed out either way); DeepSeekV2-16B dropped 21.4 points under explained vs. 13.2 under direct.
Weaker models (CodeLlama, StarCoder2, Llama3.2-3B) showed zero improvement on SecurityEval under either feedback type, flat 0.0 change across the board, reinforcing that repair capability tracks instruction-following strength, not just feedback quality.
The explained feedback itself was validated for quality: manual review of 160 samples found over 98% of GPT-4o's explanations were correct and actionable.
Discussion / limitations the authors name themselves (Section 6)
CodeQL as the sole detection tool: catches static vulnerabilities but misses dynamic/runtime-only issues.
Python-only: explicitly flagged as not generalizable to C++, Java, etc.
Single-turn, static evaluation: doesn't capture real iterative dev workflows where a human and model go back and forth multiple times.
Only two benchmarks, may not cover the full universe of vulnerability types.

Key Findings
RQ1. Do LLMs generate vulnerable code by default?
Tested 8 models (GPT-4o, GPT-3.5, Llama3.1/3.2, CodeLlama-7B/34B, StarCoder2-15B, DeepSeek-Coder-V2) on two benchmarks: SecurityEval (69 CWE types, 121 questions) and SecCodePLT (21 CWE types, 1,071 questions).
Vulnerability rates ranged from ~4% to ~27% depending on model and dataset.
Surprising finding: newer/larger models weren't always safer. GPT-4o was more vulnerable than GPT-3.5-turbo on both benchmarks. Same pattern for Llama3 vs. CodeLlama.
Code-optimized models (CodeLlama, StarCoder2) tended to be safer than general-purpose models, but that edge disappeared on the more diverse SecurityEval benchmark.
Models tend to fail on the same vulnerability types, suggesting shared weaknesses from training data/architecture, not random noise.
RQ2. Do self-generated vulnerability hints help prevent vulnerabilities?
Method: have the LLM predict its own top-5 likely vulnerabilities before writing code, then feed that back in as a guardrail.
Effectiveness depended entirely on hint quality:
Relevance: does the hint actually name the real (target) vulnerability?
Precision: is the hint's description technically correct?
Contextualization: is the hint grounded in the specific coding task, not just a generic CWE definition?
When hints missed the real vulnerability, they sometimes made things worse (misleading the model).
GPT-4o generated the most precise hints (98.6%); CodeLlama-7B the worst (46.5%).
Contextualized hints beat plain CWE definitions, especially on the harder SecurityEval benchmark.
RQ3. Can LLMs repair their own vulnerable code when given feedback?
Two feedback types tested, both derived from CodeQL (static analysis tool):
Direct feedback: raw CodeQL output (vulnerability type + line number).
Explained feedback: GPT-4o expands the raw output into a full explanation + actionable fix suggestions.
Explained feedback consistently outperformed direct feedback, but only for models with strong instruction-following (GPT-4o, DeepSeek-Coder-V2, GPT-3.5-turbo).
Weaker models (CodeLlama, StarCoder2, Llama3.2-3B) barely improved under either condition, seeming to lack the reasoning capacity to act on feedback, not just the information.
Suggested next steps (Discussion section)
Mixed-strategy pipelines: use smaller code-optimized models for initial generation, larger reasoning models for vulnerability repair.
Fine-grained, explained feedback matters more than raw detector output.
Expand vulnerability coverage rather than optimizing for a narrow known set.

Qwen2.5
What is it
Released by Alibaba's Qwen Team, a family of open-weight LLMs at 0.5B, 1.5B, 3B, 7B, 14B, 32B, and 72B parameters, plus two proprietary MoE variants (Qwen2.5-Turbo, Qwen2.5-Plus) hosted via Alibaba Cloud.
Big jump from the prior version: pre-training data scaled from 7 trillion to 18 trillion tokens, plus over 1 million post-training examples using supervised fine-tuning and a two-stage RL process (offline DPO + online GRPO).
Coding-specific relevance (this is the part that matters for your project)
Qwen2.5 was trained with dedicated coding data pulled from a sister model, Qwen2.5-Coder, and instruction-tuned across roughly 40 programming languages.
On coding benchmarks, Qwen2.5-72B-Instruct scores 86.6% on HumanEval and 88.2% on MBPP, and it's evaluated on MultiPL-E, which spans Python, C++, Java, PHP, TypeScript, C#, Bash, and JavaScript. That's notable because your paper was Python-only, one of its named limitations.
Performance-wise, Qwen2.5-72B-Instruct is positioned as matching or beating Llama-3.1-405B-Instruct despite being about a fifth the size, and competitive with GPT-4o-mini/GPT-4o at smaller scales.
The gap that jumps out
This entire technical report is about functional/general capability (HumanEval, MBPP, MMLU, math, multilingual, long-context, etc.). There is no security or vulnerability evaluation anywhere in it, no CWE testing, no CodeQL, nothing like TarV-R/AllV-R from your paper. So Qwen2.5 has strong coding credentials but an entirely untested secure coding profile. That's a clean, citable reason it'd be a good candidate to run through your paper's methodology.

