BRAINSTORM: Possible Topics
Does structured Chain-of-Thought reasoning (from Lakshmi's survey) outperform this paper's "self-generated hints" or "explained feedback" methods for either preventing or repairing vulnerabilities?
Their study is single-turn only; would a multi-turn, conversational repair loop (more CoT-like) close the gap for weaker models that struggled with one-shot feedback?
Everything here is Python, would findings hold for another language?
Qwen2.5 wasn't one of the 8 models tested in this paper, would it show the same insecure-by-default pattern, and could running it on the ORC cluster be a natural first hands-on experiment for us?
Do reasoning models (LRMs) that produce step-by-step chain-of-thought before answering generate less vulnerable code by default than standard LLMs, without needing self-generated hints or external feedback at all? 
What underlying factor (architecture, training data composition, or fine-tuning strategy) best explains why newer/larger models like GPT-4o are more vulnerable than predecessors like GPT-3.5, despite being more capable overall? 

